# .dockerignore -- what never leaves the host.
#
# The build context is harness/ (see container/Dockerfile), which is also where
# the operator's filled-in .env lives. The Dockerfile copies only
# container/requirements.lock and workspace/, so no secret could reach the
# image -- but the whole context is still uploaded to the docker daemon, and a
# secret that never leaves the host is a better secret than one that merely
# fails to be copied.
#
# run/ is excluded for the same reason from the other direction: it holds the
# resolved workspaces, and a resolved workspace carries the research question.
# The image is built from the unresolved workspace/ and must stay question-free
# -- the loop overwrites the seed with the resolved copy at run start.
#
# The rest of this list is noise reduction: a smaller context is a faster build
# and, more usefully, a build whose layer cache is not invalidated by an
# unrelated log file.

# Secrets. .env.example is documentation and is not copied anywhere either way.
.env
.env.*

# Resolved runs, staged data, and everything a run or a collection left behind.
run/
data/
logs/
cache/
crux-collect-*/
*.eval
*.timeline.jsonl
*.tar.gz

# Version control and editor droppings.
.git
.gitignore
.DS_Store

# Python bytecode and the host-side venv from running the loop in place.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
.venv/
